Rast <rast2@hotmail.com> said:

> Take Pacman as an example.  You get points for eating dots, points for 
> eating ghosts, and points for eating the occasional fruit.  The game 
> ends when you run out of lives.  The scoring system is well balanced; 
> if two people each play one game, the one who scored higher played 
> better, and even the loser will acknowledge that (or complain about the 
> joystick).

To extend this analogy: Pacman doesn't track conducts, but could. What
if a player cleared every level while eating:

* no big ghost-turning dot
* every big dot but no ghosts
* every big dot and all 16 ghosts
* no fruit
* every fruit
* and never using a tunnel

and so on? Some of these conducts diminish the raw Pacman score per
level, and many likely diminish the total number of levels won, even
though they reflect a higher playing skill. A skilled conduct-keeping
player could easily lose to a less-skilled conduct-ignoring player.

In this analogy then, should Pacman alter its score to reflect these
conducts? How would it fairly do so? And consider that -- compared to
NetHack -- Pacman has a much simpler and less dimensional game space
for mapping to a single score.


Personally, I believe creating a fair distillation of NetHack's
N-dimensional game space into a single and fairly ordered score point
is effectively an unsolvable problem. At the same time, I understand
the lure of the quest to solve this problem :^)


